L = {}
L["red-sea"] = """Israel had walked out of Egypt after the last plague. God did not lead them by the short road to Canaan, which went through Philistine land. He said they might change their minds if they faced war and want to go back to Egypt. So he led them the long way, toward the Red Sea. By day the Lord went ahead of them in a pillar of cloud, and by night in a pillar of fire. He was literally leading them.

But then God told them to turn back and camp by the sea, with water in front of them. It looked like a mistake. Meanwhile, back in Egypt, Pharaoh changed his mind. What have we done? We have let the Israelites go and have lost their services! He got his chariots ready and took 600 of the best, along with all the other chariots of Egypt. He chased after Israel, and caught up with them while they were camped by the sea.

The Israelites looked up and saw the Egyptians marching after them. They were terrified and cried out to the Lord. Then they turned on Moses. Was it because there were no graves in Egypt that you brought us to the desert to die? What have you done to us by bringing us out of Egypt? Didn't we say to you in Egypt, leave us alone, let us serve the Egyptians? It would have been better for us to serve the Egyptians than to die in the desert!

Moses gave one of the great answers in the Bible. Do not be afraid. Stand firm and you will see the deliverance the Lord will bring you today. The Egyptians you see today you will never see again. The Lord will fight for you. You need only to be still.

God said to Moses, why are you crying out to me? Tell the Israelites to move on. Raise your staff and stretch out your hand over the sea to divide the water, so that the Israelites can go through the sea on dry ground. The angel of God, who had been traveling in front of Israel's army, moved behind them. The pillar of cloud moved too, and stood between the Egyptians and Israel. It brought darkness to one side and light to the other, so neither went near the other all night.

Then Moses stretched out his hand over the sea. All that night the Lord drove the sea back with a strong east wind and turned it into dry land. The waters were divided, and the Israelites went through the sea on dry ground, with a wall of water on their right and on their left.

The Egyptians pursued them, all Pharaoh's horses, chariots, and horsemen, into the sea. During the last watch of the night, God threw the Egyptian army into confusion. The wheels of their chariots came off, so that they had difficulty driving. They said, let's flee from the Israelites! The Lord is fighting for them against Egypt.

Then the Lord told Moses to stretch out his hand again. At daybreak the sea went back to its place. The Egyptians were fleeing toward it, and the Lord swept them into the sea. Not one of them survived. The Israelites saw the Egyptians lying dead on the shore. The people feared the Lord and put their trust in him and in Moses.

Why does this matter? It is the greatest rescue in the Old Testament, and the one that every later writer points back to. The Psalms, the prophets, and the New Testament all say, remember the sea. It tells us that God makes a way where there is none.

It also tells us that this wasn't a rescue where Israel did a lot. They were told to stand still. The Lord himself fought for them. Later the New Testament uses the crossing as a picture of baptism, going through the water from slavery to freedom.

What happened after? Moses and the people sang a song. I will sing to the Lord, for he is highly exalted. Miriam, Moses' sister, took a tambourine, and all the women followed her, dancing. But three days later the people were already complaining about the lack of water. Freedom was real, but learning to trust God took longer.

What does this mean for us today? You may find yourself with the sea in front of you and the enemy behind you. Everything says it's over. God sometimes leads us right to the place where we're cornered, so that we know it is him who saves.

And the instruction is still the same. Be still. That doesn't mean do nothing. Israel did walk through the sea. But it means that the outcome doesn't depend on us.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God's way of rescue often goes through the very thing we fear. The waters that looked like a dead end became the road to freedom."""

L["sinai"] = """Three months after leaving Egypt, the people of Israel reached the Desert of Sinai and camped in front of the mountain. Moses went up to God, and God called to him from the mountain. Tell the people what I did to Egypt. You yourselves have seen how I carried you on eagles' wings and brought you to myself. Now if you obey me fully and keep my covenant, then out of all nations you will be my treasured possession. Although the whole earth is mine, you will be for me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.

Notice the order. God didn't say, obey me and I will rescue you. He had already rescued them. He said, I carried you to myself. The law was not the price of freedom. It was how a free people would live with their God. And the people answered together. We will do everything the Lord has said.

God told Moses to get the people ready. They were to wash their clothes and be ready on the third day. Boundaries were set around the mountain. No one was allowed to touch it. On the morning of the third day there was thunder and lightning, with a thick cloud over the mountain, and a very loud trumpet blast. Everyone in the camp trembled. Moses led the people out of the camp to meet God, and they stood at the foot of the mountain.

Mount Sinai was covered with smoke, because the Lord descended on it in fire. The smoke billowed up from it like smoke from a furnace, and the whole mountain trembled violently. As the sound of the trumpet grew louder and louder, Moses spoke and the voice of God answered him.

Then God spoke all the words of the Ten Commandments. I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of Egypt, out of the land of slavery. You shall have no other gods before me. No idols. Don't misuse my name. Remember the Sabbath day. Honor your father and mother. Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony. Do not covet.

The people were terrified. When they saw the thunder and lightning and heard the trumpet and saw the mountain in smoke, they trembled with fear. They stayed at a distance and said to Moses, speak to us yourself and we will listen. But do not have God speak to us or we will die. Moses said, do not be afraid. God has come to test you, so that the fear of God will be with you to keep you from sinning.

Moses then went up the mountain for forty days and forty nights. God gave him more laws and told him how to build a tent where he would live among them. The Tabernacle was to be built exactly as God showed him on the mountain.

Why does this matter? Sinai is where Israel became a nation under God. The Ten Commandments are the center of all the laws that follow. They show how to love God and how to love other people. Jesus summed them up in two. Love the Lord your God with all your heart, and love your neighbor as yourself.

It is also the first time in history that a whole people heard God's voice directly. The people of Israel were not told about God by a priest. They stood at the foot of the mountain, in fear and wonder.

What happened after? While Moses was up on the mountain, the people grew impatient and made a golden calf, and broke the covenant almost before it was made. That is the next scene. Even so, God did not give up on them. He gave the law a second time, and he came to live among them.

What does this mean for us today? God's commands are not meant to take our freedom. They came after freedom, from a God who had already rescued his people. They are how a free person stays free. Without them, we fall into slavery again.

It also reminds us that God is holy, and that he is not to be treated lightly. But there is a promise too. At Sinai the people stayed at a distance, afraid. Through Jesus, we can come close. Hebrews says we have not come to a mountain that burns with fire, but to Mount Zion, the city of the living God.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us that God gave us a way to live, and that he did it out of love. The law says, here is how to live with the God who saved you."""

L["tablets"] = """Moses had been on top of Mount Sinai for forty days. The people at the bottom grew restless. They said to Aaron, come, make us gods who will go before us. As for this fellow Moses who brought us up out of Egypt, we don't know what has happened to him. They wanted something they could see.

Aaron said, take off the gold earrings that your wives, sons, and daughters are wearing, and bring them to me. He melted the gold, formed it with a tool, and made it into an idol cast in the shape of a calf. Then they said, these are your gods, Israel, who brought you up out of Egypt. Aaron saw this and built an altar in front of it, and announced, tomorrow there will be a festival to the Lord. So the next day the people got up early, sacrificed burnt offerings, and sat down to eat and drink. Then they got up to indulge in revelry.

Up on the mountain, God told Moses, go down, because your people have become corrupt. They have been quick to turn away from what I commanded. He said he would destroy them and make a great nation from Moses. But Moses did something remarkable. He interceded for the people. He reminded God of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. And the Lord relented.

Moses went down the mountain with the two tablets of the covenant law in his hands. They were written on both sides, the work of God himself. When Joshua heard the noise of the people shouting, he thought it was the sound of war. Moses said, it is not the sound of victory, it is not the sound of defeat. It is the sound of singing that I hear.

When Moses approached the camp and saw the calf and the dancing, his anger burned. He threw the tablets out of his hands, breaking them to pieces at the foot of the mountain. Then he took the calf they had made and burned it in the fire. He ground it to powder, scattered it on the water, and made the Israelites drink it.

He said to Aaron, what did these people do to you, that you led them into such great sin? Aaron's answer was weak. They gave me the gold, and I threw it into the fire, and out came this calf. As if it had happened by itself.

Moses stood at the entrance to the camp and called, whoever is for the Lord, come to me. The Levites gathered around him. About three thousand people died that day. Then the next day, Moses told the people, you have committed a great sin. But now I will go up to the Lord, and perhaps I can make atonement for your sin. He went back up the mountain and prayed. Please forgive their sin, but if not, then blot me out of the book you have written.

God didn't blot him out. He told Moses to cut two new stone tablets, and he wrote the words on them again. Then the Lord passed in front of Moses and proclaimed, the Lord, the Lord, the compassionate and gracious God, slow to anger, abounding in love and faithfulness, maintaining love to thousands, and forgiving wickedness, rebellion and sin.

Why does this matter? The tablets broke because the covenant was broken, and almost as soon as it was made. It showed that the people could not keep the law by themselves. But it also shows that God does not walk away. The second set of tablets was written by the same hand. This is what grace looks like in the Old Testament.

It also shows what an intercessor does. Moses stood in the gap between a holy God and a sinful people. The New Testament says that Jesus does this for us, and that he is always living to intercede for us.

What happened after? The people repented, and God told them to build the Tabernacle. His presence came down and filled it. He lived among them even after they had failed him.

What does this mean for us today? Most of us have our own golden calf, something we turn to when God seems silent. We want something we can see. Aaron's story shows how quickly we can talk ourselves into it, and how thin the excuses are.

But the good news of this chapter is God's self-description. Compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in love. When we fail, his first move is not to destroy. It is to forgive, and to give us the words again.

That is why this story is so important. A broken covenant and a broken set of tablets was not the end. God made it new. That is what Jesus did for everyone, with a new covenant in his blood."""

L["serpent"] = """Israel had been wandering in the desert for almost forty years. They had left Egypt, crossed the sea, and received the law at Sinai. But they had refused to enter the Promised Land because they were afraid of the people who lived there. So God had said that the whole generation would die in the wilderness, and that their children would enter instead. Now that time was almost up. Aaron had died and been buried on Mount Hor, and the people were traveling around the land of Edom, the long way.

The people grew impatient on the road. They began to speak against God and against Moses. Why have you brought us up out of Egypt to die in the wilderness? There is no bread! There is no water! And we detest this miserable food. The miserable food was the manna, the bread God had given them every morning for almost forty years.

Think about what they were saying. God had saved them from slavery, split the sea, and fed them every day with bread from heaven. And they called it miserable. They had forgotten who they were dealing with. It is easy to do.

Then the Lord sent venomous snakes among the people. They bit the people, and many Israelites died. The people came to Moses and said, we sinned when we spoke against the Lord and against you. Pray that the Lord will take the snakes away from us. So Moses prayed for the people.

The Lord said to Moses, make a snake and put it up on a pole. Anyone who is bitten can look at it and live. So Moses made a bronze snake and put it up on a pole. Then when anyone was bitten by a snake and looked at the bronze snake, they lived.

Think about how strange that is. God did not take the snakes away. He didn't give them an antidote or a medicine. He told them to look at a bronze snake on a pole. They had to believe that something that looked like the problem could be the cure. The only thing they could do was look and trust. If they refused to look, they died.

Why does this matter? Jesus talked about this story himself, in one of the most famous conversations in the Bible. He was talking to a man named Nicodemus at night. He said, just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up, that everyone who believes may have eternal life in him. Then he said the verse that is known all over the world. For God so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him shall not perish but have eternal life.

The bronze snake was a picture of Jesus on the cross. Snakes meant sin and death. Jesus took on our sin, and became the thing that kills us, so that we could live. Everyone who looks at him in faith is healed.

What happened after? The Israelites kept the bronze snake. Hundreds of years later, they started to worship it. King Hezekiah broke it into pieces, because it had become an idol. The thing God had used to heal them was never meant to be worshiped.

What does this mean for us today? When we are bitten by the poison of our own mistakes, God doesn't ask us to fix ourselves first. He asks us to look. That is what faith is. It is not a complicated thing. It is turning toward the one God has lifted up.

It also warns us about complaining. The people were bitten right after they complained about God's gift. A thankful heart is a safe place to live.

That is why this story is so important. It is the clearest picture in the Old Testament of how a person is saved. Not by doing, but by looking. Fix your eyes on Jesus."""

L["balaam"] = """Israel was almost at the end of its journey. The people were camped on the plains of Moab, just east of the Jordan River. Balak, the king of Moab, was terrified. He had heard what Israel had done to the Amorites, and he thought they were too many for him. So he sent for a man named Balaam, a well-known prophet who lived far away on the Euphrates. Balak told him, come and put a curse on these people. I know that whoever you bless is blessed, and whoever you curse is cursed.

Balaam asked God. God said, do not go with them. You must not put a curse on those people, because they are blessed. Balaam told the messengers to go home. But Balak sent more princes, more important and with more money. Balaam answered, even if Balak gave me his palace filled with silver and gold, I could not do anything great or small to go beyond the command of the Lord my God. But he asked them to stay the night, and again asked God. This time God said, go with them, but do only what I tell you.

The next morning, Balaam saddled his donkey and went. But God was very angry that he went, and the angel of the Lord stood in the road to oppose him. Balaam was riding on his donkey, and two servants were with him. The donkey saw the angel of the Lord standing in the road with a drawn sword in his hand. She turned off the road into a field. Balaam beat her to get her back on the road.

Then the angel stood in a narrow path between two vineyards, with walls on both sides. When the donkey saw the angel, she pressed close to the wall, crushing Balaam's foot against it. So he beat her again. Then the angel moved ahead and stood in a narrow place where there was no room to turn. This time the donkey lay down under Balaam. He was furious and beat her with his staff.

Then the Lord opened the donkey's mouth, and she said to Balaam, what have I done to you to make you beat me these three times? Balaam answered, you have made a fool of me! If I had a sword in my hand, I would kill you right now. The donkey said, am I not your own donkey, which you have always ridden, to this day? Have I been in the habit of doing this to you? No, he said.

Then the Lord opened Balaam's eyes, and he saw the angel standing in the road with his sword drawn. He bowed low and fell facedown. The angel asked him why he had beaten his donkey. The donkey saw me and turned away three times. If she had not turned away, I would certainly have killed you by now, and I would have spared her. Balaam admitted, I have sinned.

The angel told Balaam to go on, but to speak only what he was told. Balak took Balaam up to high places to look down at Israel and curse them. Three times, Balaam tried to speak, and three times God put blessings in his mouth instead. How can I curse those whom God has not cursed? Balak was furious. I summoned you to curse my enemies, but you have blessed them these three times!

One of the blessings looks far into the future. I see him, but not now. I behold him, but not near. A star will come out of Jacob. A scepter will rise out of Israel. Christians have long seen this as pointing to Jesus. It may also be why wise men from the east followed a star to Bethlehem.

Why does this matter? It shows that no one can curse the people God has blessed. Balak had the money, the army, and a famous prophet, and it didn't matter. It also shows that God can speak through anyone, even a donkey.

What happened after? Sadly, Balaam later found another way to hurt Israel. He advised Moab to lure the Israelites into idol worship. The New Testament uses Balaam as a warning about people who love money more than they love God. Jude and Peter both mention him. Balaam in the end was killed in battle.

What does this mean for us today? The donkey saw what the prophet could not. Sometimes the people or things we overlook see God's hand before the experts do. And sometimes God uses interruptions, like the donkey stopping in the road, to save us from something worse.

It also tells us that God's blessing on his people cannot be undone by anyone's words. Whoever belongs to him is safe from curses. You may feel that people have spoken against you, or that the odds are against you. But what God has blessed, no one can curse.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us that God protects his people, even from enemies they don't know about. They were safe because of him, not because of anything they had done."""
